    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Since we want $f(x)=f^{-1}(x)$, then $f(f^{-1}(x))=x$. Since the inverse of a function $f$ is defined as $f(f^{-1}(x))=x$, this is true by definition, so all $x$s satisfy this condition.\n\nTherefore, in order to find the specific value(s) of $x$ for which the condition holds true, we lets $f(x)=y$ and desired $f(y)$ to be back in terms of $x$. Following this logic, we want $f(y)=x$ instead.\n\nLet $y=f^{-1}(x)$. So, $f(y)=3y-8$. Setting this expression equal to $x$, we have:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n3y-8 &= x\\\\\n3f^{-1}(x)-8 &= x\\\\\n3f^{-1}(x) &= x+8\\\\\nf^{-1}(x)&=\\frac{x+8}{3}\n\\end{align*}\n\nApplying $f^{-1}(x)$ to both sides, we find:\n\n\\begin{align*}\nx &= 3x-8\\\\\n2x &= 8\\\\\nx&=4\n\\end{align*}\n\nThus, the solution is $\\boxed{x=4}$.",
